-// moe-zc: "zero-copy" expert cache for MoE models that do not fit in VRAM.
+// moe-zc: hybrid GPU+CPU execution of MoE experts for models that do not fit in VRAM.
 // Part of the ai "LLM compiler" project (fork of llama.cpp, MIT).
 //
 // The idea in plain words
 // -----------------------
-// All expert weights stay in pinned (page-locked) host RAM. The GPU can read
-// that memory directly over PCIe ("zero-copy"), so every expert is always
-// usable by a GPU kernel without any copy and without asking the CPU.
+// All expert weights stay in pinned host RAM. The experts used often *right now*
+// also get a copy in a VRAM "slot" (a cache). For every expert weight tensor we
+// keep a small table in GPU memory: table[e] = address of expert e's VRAM copy,
+// or nullptr if expert e is not in VRAM.
 //
-// Reading over PCIe is ~20x slower than reading VRAM, so the experts that are
-// used often *right now* get a copy in a VRAM "slot". For every expert weight
-// tensor we keep a small table in GPU memory: table[e] = where expert e is
-// read from (its VRAM slot, or its place in host RAM). The matrix-vector
-// kernel (mmvq.cu) looks the address up in this table.
+// When a layer runs (decode, <= ZC_MAX_TOKENS tokens):
+//   * the GPU matrix-vector kernels (mmvq.cu) compute the experts found in VRAM
+//     and skip the others (table entry nullptr);
+//   * at the same time CPU worker threads compute the skipped ("cold") experts
+//     straight from RAM: the CPU reads RAM at ~76 GB/s, far faster than the GPU
+//     could pull the same weights over PCIe (~22 GB/s);
+//   * the GPU waits for the CPU's results and puts them where the skipped
+//     experts' outputs belong; the rest of llama.cpp's graph is unchanged.
 //
-// Which experts deserve a slot is decided on the CPU, after each generated
-// token, from the experts that token used (every miss is copied in; the
-// victim is chosen by recency and frequency, see zc_crf). New copies are uploaded on a separate CUDA stream while the GPU is
-// already computing the next token, so the CPU never sits on the critical
-// path of a token. This replaces llama.cpp's --moe-cache-mib, which stops the
-// GPU in every layer to ask the CPU which experts to upload.
+// GPU and CPU talk through a "mailbox" in pinned host memory that both can
+// read and write: a tiny GPU kernel writes the request flag, the CPU threads
+// spin on it; the CPU writes the done flag, a tiny GPU kernel spins on it.
+// No CUDA API call is made by the CPU during a token, so the whole token stays
+// one CUDA graph; a round trip costs ~4 us (measured, tools/pingpong).
 //
-// Safety rules (the GPU may read the tables at any moment):
-//  * a table entry always points to valid data: first the data is uploaded,
-//    then (later on the same stream) the table entry is changed;
-//  * an evicted slot is not overwritten until the token that might still be
-//    reading it through an old table entry has finished ("quarantine").
+// Which experts get a VRAM slot is decided after each step from the experts the
+// step used (replacement policy CRF adapted from llama.cpp's MoE cache). New
+// copies are uploaded on a separate stream during the next token; table
+// changes are published only between steps, so GPU kernels and CPU threads of
+// one step always see the same split into "VRAM" and "CPU" experts.
 
